Peace and quiet?  ;)

Or, more realistically, being able to generate complex ideas, refine
them, and produce them without constantly having to deal with people 
second-guessing you every step of the way... and more importantly, without
having people who don't understand the big picture second-guessing you.
There's also the subset of players who review your code with a fine-toothed comb
and say "Well, _I_ would have done it _THIS_ way, and that would've been much 
better", but fortunately those can be safely ignored unless they turn up
an actual bug, and then you can just fix the bug and move on.

It is a much lower-stress environment if you don't let anyone see inside
the process, but I don't think the advantages outweigh the problems that
can arise.

